= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Interpret the map as the action on $\mathbb C^n$. A <smooth representation> requires every vector to have an open stabilizer in the <locally profinite group> $\operatorname{GL}_n(F)$. Take the first standard basis vector $e_1$. Every identity neighbourhood contains matrices $d_a=\operatorname{diag}(1+a,1,\ldots,1)$ with $a\ne0$ sufficiently small in the <local field>. We may require $|a|<1$, so $1+a\ne0$. Injectivity of the <field embedding> gives
$$
\pi(d_a)e_1=(1+\iota(a))e_1\ne e_1.
$$
Thus no identity neighbourhood fixes $e_1$, and its stabilizer is not open. Consequently **the representation is not smooth for any $n\ge1$**. This includes $n=1$, when a nonzero vector's stabilizer is just the identity. The field embedding need not be continuous, but no continuity assumption would repair this stabilizer obstruction. The existence of the stated embedding itself forces characteristic zero; in positive characteristic no field embedding into $\mathbb C$ exists.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

An $\ell$-group here means a <locally profinite group>, so identity neighbourhoods contain <compact-open subgroups>. The right action is a <group representation> because $\rho(g)\rho(h)f(x)=f(xgh)=\rho(gh)f(x)$.

Let $f$ be a <compactly supported locally constant function> with support $S$. For every $x\in S$, local constancy supplies a <compact-open subgroup> $K_x$ with $f$ constant on $xK_x$, and this coset lies in $S$. Compactness gives a finite cover $S=\bigcup_{j=1}^r x_jK_j$. Put $K=\bigcap_jK_j$. Each coset in this cover is right $K$-invariant, so $S$ is right $K$-invariant. Within the support, $xk$ and $x$ lie in the same chosen $x_jK_j$ and have the same function value; outside the support, right $K$-invariance of $S$ ensures that both values remain zero. Hence $\rho(k)f=f$ for all $k\in K$. The zero function is fixed by every subgroup. This proves that the <smooth right regular representation> is smooth.

For a fixed <compact-open subgroup> $K$, right-invariant functions are functions on the discrete coset space $G/K$. Compact support becomes finite support there, since the image of a compact set in a discrete space is finite. Conversely every finitely supported function on $G/K$ lifts to a compactly supported locally constant function on $G$. Thus
$$
\boxed{V^K\cong\mathbb C^{(G/K)},}
$$
where the superscript indicates finite support. Its basis is the set of characteristic functions $1_{gK}$, and its dimension is finite exactly when $[G:K]$ is finite.

If $G$ is compact, the discrete quotient $G/K$ is compact and therefore finite for every such $K$; hence the representation is <admissible>. Conversely, admissibility gives finite index for any one <compact-open subgroup>. Then $G$ is a finite union of compact cosets, so is compact. Therefore **the representation is admissible if and only if $G$ is compact**.

