= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $U_m=1+\mathfrak p^m$, $m\ge1$, for the <principal-unit filtration of a local field>. Each <linear character> $\chi_i$ is a <smooth character>, since its kernel contains $U_{i+1}$. A vector in the algebraic <direct sum> has only finitely many nonzero coordinates. Taking $m$ larger than all their indices gives a common subgroup $U_m$ fixing the vector, so the <direct sum> is smooth.

If $i<m$, then $U_m\subseteq U_{i+1}$, so $V_i$ is fixed by $U_m$. If $i\ge m$, the subgroup $U_i$ lies inside $U_m$, and $\chi_i$ is nontrivial on $U_i$; hence $V_i^{U_m}=0$. It follows that
$$
\boxed{V^{U_m}=\bigoplus_{1\le i<m}V_i,\qquad\dim V^{U_m}=m-1.}
$$
Every <compact-open subgroup> $K$ of $F^\times$ contains some $U_m$, because it is an identity neighbourhood. Therefore $V^K\subseteq V^{U_m}$ is finite-dimensional. **The <direct sum> is an admissible representation**, despite its infinite total dimension.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <algebraic dual of a direct sum> is a product, rather than another <direct sum>. The map
$$
R:V^*\longrightarrow\prod_{i\ge1}V_i^*,\qquad\ell\longmapsto(\ell|_{V_i})_i
$$
is injective, since the summands span $V$. Given any family $(\ell_i)_i$, define $\ell((v_i)_i)=\sum_i\ell_i(v_i)$. This sum is finite for each vector, regardless of whether the family of functionals has finite support. It defines a <linear functional> and is inverse to $R$. Hence this is a natural vector-space isomorphism.

The <algebraic contragredient representation> is $(\pi^*(g)\ell)(v)=\ell(\pi(g^{-1})v)$. On the $i$th coordinate,
$$
(\pi^*(g)\ell)_i(v_i)=\ell_i(\chi_i(g)^{-1}v_i)=\chi_i(g)^{-1}\ell_i(v_i).
$$
Thus $R$ intertwines the actions and gives
$$
\boxed{(V^*,\pi^*)\cong\prod_{i\ge1}(V_i^*,\chi_i^{-1}).}
$$
No smoothness restriction has yet been imposed on that product.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $\ell=(\ell_i)_i$ be a <smooth vector> in the product description of the <algebraic contragredient representation>. Its open stabilizer contains $U_m$ for some $m$. For every $i\ge m$, choose $u_i\in U_i\subseteq U_m$ with $\chi_i(u_i)\ne1$. Invariance of $\ell$ at its $i$th coordinate gives
$$
\chi_i(u_i)^{-1}\ell_i=\ell_i,
$$
forcing $\ell_i=0$. Hence a smooth functional has finite support, contained among $i<m$.

Conversely a finitely supported family has indices bounded by some $N$. Each inverse <linear character> has the same kernel as its original <linear character>, and all those kernels contain $U_{N+1}$. Thus this subgroup fixes the family. The <smooth dual> is therefore
$$
\boxed{V^\vee=\bigoplus_{i\ge1}V_i^*.}
$$
This is the principle that <conductors force finite support in a smooth dual>. For instance a product vector with every coordinate nonzero is a perfectly valid algebraic functional on the original <direct sum>, but is not smooth.

